Patina backend installer
========================

This package supplies a per-user installer and a standalone candidate. It does
not enable a service, select a runtime, or replace files owned by Patina Desktop.

The command is patina-backend. The candidate is installed at:
  /usr/lib/patina-backend-installer/candidate

Use the candidate's manifest digest from verified delivery evidence. Staging
copies and verifies its files into a private, persistent user runtime directory:

  patina-backend --stage-runtime /usr/lib/patina-backend-installer/candidate \
    --runtime-root /absolute/user-owned/runtime --manifest-sha256 DIGEST
  patina-backend --inspect-runtime --runtime-root /absolute/user-owned/runtime
  patina-backend --select-runtime DIGEST --runtime-root /absolute/user-owned/runtime \
    --expected-current none
  patina-backend --activate-runtime DIGEST --runtime-root /absolute/user-owned/runtime

Use the observed current manifest instead of "none" when upgrading. For local
debug candidates, add --allow-debug explicitly to those commands. Existing
DEB/AppImage services require the source confirmation described in the repository's
Linux development guide. Do not copy the service template over a live unit.

Activation runs as the current user and preserves existing login preferences.
It requires that user's systemd manager and matching profile roots. Use a durable
directory under user data storage; a real unit's PrivateTmp makes host /tmp paths
unsuitable. API verification uses the configured backend port (default 14840).

Package upgrades only replace the installer candidate. Explicit selection and
activation control the user's running version. Removing this installer package
does not uninstall or stop a separately activated user runtime, nor remove data.
User runtime deactivation/removal is a separate operation; this development
candidate is not a complete public installation/uninstallation workflow yet.

Desktop/backend protocol compatibility, publisher authentication and supported
Linux environments require their own checks. A manifest hash is content integrity
evidence, not a publisher signature. This DEB targets Debian-family amd64 GNU
systems; the standalone TAR is a separate delivery format.
